#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203875 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de17e8f9-e3dc-5ba0-b6c9-327aa8cc10e0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Best One Dimensional Reduction Given SVD of Data"}] :question/id #uuid "d6f2c4b7-eb2d-5d32-bcd8-8ec9443c14da" :question/math-academy-id "e-18786" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4 \\times 2$ matrix\n![](images/23/232f6bffd41437d85b7a0f09ec268d459009656ef77fba4b4fb445b52dd13a1d.svg)\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X = U \\Sigma V^T$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.689 & -0.424 & 0.495 & 0.318 \\\\ 0.143 & -0.241 & -0.524 & 0.804 \\\\ 0.695 & -0.188 & 0.650 & 0.244 \\\\ -0.149 & 0.853 & 0.241 & 0.439\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.075 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.294 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.709 & -0.706 \\\\ 0.706 & -0.709\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance." :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $X^TX.$ Using the SVD $X = U\\Sigma V^T$ and the orthogonality of $U,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\Sigma^T\\Sigma$ is diagonal with the squared singular values on the diagonal, the eigenvectors of $X^TX$ are the columns of $V,$ and the eigenvalues are the squared singular values. The largest eigenvalue corresponds to the first singular value $\\sigma_1 = 2.075,$ so the first principal component is $v_1,$ the first column of $V{:}$\n\n![](images/e9/e91c75818de82c4564584df51a72432ebc97c90dd5a1c8687b24bd31998d9910.svg)\n\nTo obtain the one-dimensional version of the data, we project $X$ onto $v_1$ by computing\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X{v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1.40 & -0.62 \\\\ 0.01 & 0.43 \\\\ -0.85 & 1.19 \\\\ -0.56 & -1.00\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-0.709 \\\\ 0.706\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.43 \\\\ 0.30 \\\\ 1.44 \\\\ -0.31\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, we can compute this projection using $u_1 \\sigma_1,$ where $u_1$ is the first column of $U.$ Indeed, from the SVD,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X{v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}{v}_{1}=UΣ[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=U[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={u}_{1}{\\sigma }_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u_1$ and $\\sigma_1 = 2.075,$ we get\n\n![](images/14/148bc9bd4289245d9ada00da68f7b6e0666bfa9c3fffd47fde79164b177732d8.svg)\n\nwhich confirms the result.\n\n**Watch out!** The vector $v_1$ itself is the first principal component *direction* — a unit vector in feature space. It is not a one-dimensional representation of the data. The one-dimensional representation is $Xv_1$ (or equivalently $u_1\\sigma_1$), which is a $4 \\times 1$ vector containing the projection of each data point onto this direction."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203876 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bbf7902-3a15-5dd0-b2c8-e5b7fd541345" :knowledge-point/title "Choosing the Amount of Dimensionality Reduction for a Given Dataset"}] :question/id #uuid "a17de4fa-f7c3-5038-b9fa-03780393b360" :question/math-academy-id "e-18840" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5 \\times 3$ matrix\n![](images/83/83365da559075cf32eaebf67a12eef548f40585a84a3226aad488327de8e9e84.svg)\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X = U \\Sigma V^T$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.75 & 0.177 & 0.014 & 0.637 & -0.004 \\\\ 0.275 & -0.054 & -0.847 & 0.36 & 0.274 \\\\ -0.213 & -0.653 & 0.171 & -0.068 & 0.703 \\\\ 0.542 & -0.181 & 0.459 & 0.678 & -0.05 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.711 & 0.206 & -0.022 & 0.655\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.272 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.031 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.659 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.724 & 0.019 & 0.69 \\\\ 0.422 & 0.779 & -0.464 \\\\ 0.546 & -0.627 & -0.555\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nFill in the blanks below:\n\nWe should pick components based on \n![](images/21/21182b5f0667421011af540ed046b0924b738d74d6a09f574320bd4768df7c38.svg)\n\nIf we wish to preserve $75\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n![](images/ab/abbb30d852a431e139075cbe6686ea1aafd697e5d52351e1f23730545ea2d661.svg)." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n\\sigma_1 = 2.272, \\qquad\n\\sigma_2 = 2.031, \\qquad\n\\sigma_3 = 1.659.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sigma_1^2}{\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^3 \\sigma_i^2} =\n\\dfrac{2.272^2}{2.272^2 + 2.031^2 + 1.659^2} \\approx 42.9\\%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^2 \\sigma_i^2}{\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^3 \\sigma_i^2} =\n\\dfrac{2.272^2 + 2.031^2}{2.272^2 + 2.031^2 + 1.659^2} \\approx 77.1\\%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $42.9\\%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $77.1\\%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/f3/f35a54aa598a0c65fc14ae807b60ddd6dcb68a36dd4f0ba5089fae12ec7a9495.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to ![](images/8d/8d0e762e494aa7a29af18cae486e8fca07aa9e6fb1c68e1204b8e1765caee8c2.svg) since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $75\\%$ of the variance."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203877 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204107 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45f11c96-89e5-5662-81b6-06cff80a4cf2" :knowledge-point/title "Using PCA to Dimensionally Reduce an Uncentered Dataset"}] :question/id #uuid "dcbf83b0-b552-52d6-97d3-9bc3ac6b0823" :question/math-academy-id "e-18844" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5 \\times 3$ matrix\n\n![](images/c2/c2163de699b69fa804ad71bf683badb2e6e76115d88b444606f39e46eaa7aed9.svg)\n\nWe wish to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset while still preserving as much variance as possible.\n\nLet's walk through the process of reducing the dimensionality of the dataset." :question/worked-solution "We will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{the direction in feature space}}$ with the $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{most}}$ variance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{orthogonal}}$ to the first $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{principal component}}$ with the most variance.\n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{standardize the data}}.$\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n![](images/15/159be3f32150b2a56f4a335990cef0c71cf6362447fc4aff521751743ab2ef58.svg)\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{\\text{std}} & =UΣ{V}^{T},\\,\\text{where} \\\\ U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.688 & 0.370 & -0.421 & 0.197 & 0.417 \\\\ 0.160 & 0.258 & 0.226 & 0.910 & -0.168 \\\\ 0.642 & 0.396 & -0.101 & -0.081 & 0.644 \\\\ 0.147 & -0.753 & -0.451 & 0.353 & 0.289 \\\\ -0.260 & -0.271 & 0.747 & 0.038 & 0.547\\end{aligned}], \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.825 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.939 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.543 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.607 & 0.503 & -0.615 \\\\ -0.383 & 0.864 & 0.327 \\\\ -0.696 & -0.037 & -0.717\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2.825^2}{2.825^2 + 1.939^2 + 0.543^2} \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{66.3\\%}.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2.825^2 + 1.939^2}{2.825^2 + 1.939^2 + 0.543^2} \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{97.6\\%}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have that \n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $66.3\\%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $97.6\\%$ of the variance.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{2}$ dimensions, since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90\\%$ of the variance.\n\nWriting $v_1, v_2, v_3$ for the columns of $V,$ the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\nX_{\\text{red}} = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{X_{\\text{std}} \\cdot [v_1, v_2]},\n$$\n\nsince we need to project the data onto the first two principal components, which are the first two columns of $V.$\n\n**Note:** In order to invert the projection and interpret any further analysis in terms of the original features, we need to know the means and standard deviations of the original dataset $X.$ Additionally, in case we have more data, we can use the means and standard deviations of the original dataset to map the new data into the original feature space."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de17e8f9-e3dc-5ba0-b6c9-327aa8cc10e0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Best One Dimensional Reduction Given SVD of Data"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a907ca2-c523-5180-bcdc-bbe6f6c4642e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528372} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc0fdcf-4cb8-5018-a940-071de4027293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528645} {:answer/id #uuid "9d42bdf7-d6e9-55e1-86ce-2e629a5f8fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528677} {:answer/id #uuid "ee184618-27b0-5c25-9140-55a9d8379731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528889} {:answer/id #uuid "f805cefc-a8ea-5ac2-a5c1-3ba59d32a17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee184618-27b0-5c25-9140-55a9d8379731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528889} :answer-field/id #uuid "87da0997-109c-5d59-8556-8db99822494e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2633d33a-0706-5b9b-a985-6a0050cda4a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-355831" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.27 & 0.39 \\\\ -0.99 & -1.23 \\\\ -0.13 & -0.28 \\\\ 1.39 & 1.12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.035 & 0.86 & 0.132 & 0.491 \\\\ -0.657 & -0.314 & -0.208 & 0.653 \\\\ -0.121 & -0.196 & 0.969 & 0.092 \\\\ 0.743 & -0.351 & -0.032 & 0.569\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.389 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.542 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.707\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}0.035 \\\\ -0.657 \\\\ -0.121 \\\\ 0.743\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 2.389$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.035 \\\\ 0.86 \\\\ 0.132 \\\\ 0.491\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 2.389$" :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Notice that it represents ${u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1},$ where ${u}_{1}$ is the first column of $U$ and ${\\sigma }_{1}$ is the first singular value. But this equals  $X\\cdot {v}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cdot {v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}\\cdot {v}_{1}=UΣ\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=U\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Option II represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option III uses the values of the first row of $U$ multiplied by ${\\sigma }_{1}.$ However, the projection corresponds to the first column of $U.$ Thus, using the row vector is incorrect.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de17e8f9-e3dc-5ba0-b6c9-327aa8cc10e0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Best One Dimensional Reduction Given SVD of Data"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124dd69d-b9ac-532a-85d8-fd7b70eecf8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528342} {:answer/id #uuid "36ca5ef3-24a6-5fb1-8622-0b1d7a9443b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528441} {:answer/id #uuid "3ebbbe6b-7c84-5932-a277-a4405916dccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528460} {:answer/id #uuid "bddffe44-aacb-5f0d-a38d-a2f7fc732660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528764} {:answer/id #uuid "ff685870-d8f7-56d3-a9cf-f7dffe500056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff685870-d8f7-56d3-a9cf-f7dffe500056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528936} :answer-field/id #uuid "0408fb21-e6d7-5afd-8fc7-4e0712c6e323" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5bab4cd0-ae02-5029-8d61-df09bcee19e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-355832" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.866 & 1.212 \\\\ 0.866 & -0.173 \\\\ -0.866 & -1.212 \\\\ -0.866 & 0.173\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.670 & 0.224 & 0.224 & -0.671 \\\\ 0.224 & -0.671 & 0.671 & 0.224 \\\\ -0.670 & -0.224 & 0.224 & -0.671 \\\\ -0.224 & 0.671 & 0.671 & 0.224\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.191 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.095 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.707\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}0.866 & 1.212\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.866 & 1.212 \\\\ 0.866 & -0.173 \\\\ -0.866 & -1.212 \\\\ -0.866 & 0.173\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I represents the projection of only the first datapoint (the first row of $X$) onto the first principal component. Therefore, it produces a single scalar value rather than the full one-dimensional dataset.\n\n- Option II corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Indeed, it represents $X\\cdot {v}_{1},$ which is exactly the projection of the full dataset $X$ onto the first principal component.\n\n- Option III represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de17e8f9-e3dc-5ba0-b6c9-327aa8cc10e0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Best One Dimensional Reduction Given SVD of Data"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "158c5e57-6484-540e-8dae-1f773f268a50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528353} {:answer/id #uuid "36559b24-53e6-5745-8a45-9fd571a78363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528438} {:answer/id #uuid "4c09282e-d0f3-5c75-9b06-861585a630c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528489} {:answer/id #uuid "968f6982-b7e0-5819-8694-ea0749ae1d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #