$0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.583 & -0.434 & 0.687 & 0.016 \\\\ 0.49 & -0.561 & 0.046 & 0.666 \\\\ 0.506 & 0.466 & 0.725 & -0.029 \\\\ -0.405 & 0.529 & -0.027 & 0.746\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}1.917 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.528 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.709 & 0.706 \\\\ -0.706 & 0.709\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}-0.583 \\\\ 0.49 \\\\ 0.506 \\\\ -0.405\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 1.917$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.709 \\\\ -0.706\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}-1.26 & 0.32 \\\\ 0.06 & -1.27 \\\\ 1.19 & -0.18 \\\\ 0.02 & 1.12\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.709 \\\\ -0.706\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Notice that it represents ${u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1},$ where ${u}_{1}$ is the first column of $U$ and ${\\sigma }_{1}$ is the first singular value. But this equals $X\\cdot {v}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cdot {v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}\\cdot {v}_{1}=UΣ\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=U\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Option II represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option III corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Indeed, it represents $X\\cdot {v}_{1},$ which is exactly the projection of $X$ onto the first principal component.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de17e8f9-e3dc-5ba0-b6c9-327aa8cc10e0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Best One Dimensional Reduction Given SVD of Data"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f38a1d5-5e26-5720-933a-074463f63291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528462} {:answer/id #uuid "8eed707b-6371-5999-839b-4f5cd51042db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528642} {:answer/id #uuid "93bad0d2-4d8d-5501-8d17-ee62bd3e4da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528651} {:answer/id #uuid "9a843bc1-b154-5a46-a21d-415e36ea992a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db