d}0.29 \\\\ -0.469 \\\\ 0.676 \\\\ -0.49\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 2.065$" :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option II corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Indeed, it represents $X\\cdot {v}_{1},$ which is exactly the projection of $X$ onto the first principal component.\n\n- Option III corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Notice that it represents ${u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1},$ where ${u}_{1}$ is the first column of $U$ and ${\\sigma }_{1}$ is the first singular value. But this equals  $X\\cdot {v}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cdot {v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}\\cdot {v}_{1}=UΣ\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=U\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bbf7902-3a15-5dd0-b2c8-e5b7fd541345" :knowledge-point/title "Choosing the Amount of Dimensionality Reduction for a Given Dataset"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "780b7db4-aa11-5ca7-beff-a5f093f3ef15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528579} {:answer/id #uuid "8b461c20-1050-5d9a-91cd-8bc9ef709386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528633} {:answer/id #uuid "b8fe6545-9475-5823-b8b7-1c317fcf1b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528752} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6031ed-ea6d-5e9d-a2e7-a3897dbc6f57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd6031ed-ea6d-5e9d-a2e7-a3897dbc6f57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528803} :answer-field/id #uuid "0104538f-abdf-5164-878b-46a18f047fce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528937} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2646603c-69ca-578d-954e-dddbf2e2cb50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528399} {:answer/id #uuid "32fc720c-133a-5c75-a08a-0c1b2ac177b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528425} {:answer/id #uuid "e85b414d-9378-5c62-830c-20363052d009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32fc720c-133a-5c75-a08a-0c1b2ac177b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528425} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4176f7b-81bc-51fd-bd74-a22248865cb9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6804c35-f8e1-5715-b170-24ee05ad3186" :question/math-academy-id "q-355842" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.42 & -0.33 & -0.4 \\\\ -0.94 & 1.07 & -0.08 \\\\ 1.11 & 0.12 & 1.71 \\\\ -0.79 & -1.51 & -0.9 \\\\ 1.04 & 0.66 & -0.32\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.244 & 0.013 & 0.022 & -0.603 & 0.759 \\\\ -0.051 & -0.763 & 0.397 & 0.405 & 0.307 \\\\ 0.671 & 0.439 & 0.394 & 0.235 & 0.383 \\\\ -0.644 & 0.452 & 0.021 & 0.57 & 0.237 \\\\ 0.272 & -0.144 & -0.828 & 0.305 & 0.355\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.730 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.751 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.210 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.617 & 0.395 & -0.68 \\\\ 0.461 & -0.882 & -0.095 \\\\ 0.638 & 0.255 & 0.727\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $85%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=2.730,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=1.751,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1.210.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.730}^{2}}{{2.730}^{2}+{1.751}^{2}+{1.210}^{2}}≈62.2%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.730}^{2}+{1.751}^{2}}{{2.730}^{2}+{1.751}^{2}+{1.210}^{2}}≈87.8%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $62.2%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $87.8%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/b2/b2f5cc8cae6fac8d24e20c045990d39d088a34df26402a7f1e5137070579de03.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $85%$ of the variance."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bbf7902-3a15-5dd0-b2c8-e5b7fd541345" :knowledge-point/title "Choosing the Amount of Dimensionality Reduction for a Given Dataset"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aa5625e-af4e-5cb0-be77-41320df40a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528373} {:answer/id #uuid "29775c65-a127-562d-905b-ce5a0a1506c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528403} {:answer/id #uuid "bd240f70-4ee4-553a-b8a1-f11f13074026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd240f70-4ee4-553a-b8a1-f11f13074026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528760} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c6296bc-3942-5538-b3c5-b580c49e804f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528946} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3fa802e-1a7c-5131-9274-0f3fd251222c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528697} {:answer/id #uuid "abe5576c-3f57-582e-a2f6-b681ef2c6ece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528717} {:answer/id #uuid "ba0f7340-726e-57e2-8594-d7697b1e2ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528754} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa5d643-96f3-5f51-b2d9-8f6ea8ab456c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba0f7340-726e-57e2-8594-d7697b1e2ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528754} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dfae55b-dcf9-5c8f-99dd-37e10549a26b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3158298f-c612-536d-8b25-00f57fa2f77e" :question/math-academy-id "q-355844" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.74 & 0.61 & 0.89 \\\\ 0.38 & 0 & 0.61 \\\\ -0.92 & -0.03 & -0.87 \\\\ -1.2 & -1.61 & -1.28 \\\\ 1.01 & 1.02 & 0.65\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.389 & 0.167 & -0.312 & 0.84 & -0.133 \\\\ -0.174 & 0.453 & -0.384 & -0.192 & 0.762 \\\\ 0.321 & -0.749 & -0.348 & 0.232 & 0.402 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.384 & 0.359 & 0.419 & 0.22 \\\\ -0.464 & -0.242 & 0.712 & 0.166 & 0.438\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.329 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.311 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.591 & 0.31 & 0.745 \\\\ -0.558 & -0.823 & -0.1 \\\\ -0.582 & 0.475 & -0.66\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $95%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=3.329,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=0.9,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=0.311.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.329}^{2}}{{3.329}^{2}+{0.9}^{2}+{0.311}^{2}}≈92.4%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.329}^{2}+{0.9}^{2}}{{3.329}^{2}+{0.9}^{2}+{0.311}^{2}}≈99.2%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $92.4%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $99.2%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/e2/e2af29d0509f7ae3e3826cd35d3190b478e9c2415c00fc0e3798800d33381c59.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $95%$ of the variance."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bbf7902-3a15-5dd0-b2c8-e5b7fd541345" :knowledge-point/title "Choosing the Amount of Dimensionality Reduction for a Given Dataset"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6f25b04-4b91-598e-a565-be3663d6a848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203562} {:answer/id #uuid "c72dffd8-1d94-5753-ab91-0de31a1bf0dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203563} {:answer/id #uuid "5c4134ab-9707-5b12-b3b2-b92ef0ef55b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203564} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1c7755-a2c7-544b-8147-4c1e1d7335fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6f25b04-4b91-598e-a565-be3663d6a848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203562} :answer-field/id #uuid "673f2cfc-466c-5530-bf38-03cc0d0b363a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203561} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e228fd-1f0b-50d7-9082-a4911c109df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528404} {:answer/id #uuid "38b85c46-47af-5d2f-a564-e9efc73d7eec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528444} {:answer/id #uuid "63ef7549-c7c2-58ca-92bb-1912806cb967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528536} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd67190-901e-54cf-963f-8fa2f4f87575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbd67190-901e-54cf-963f-8fa2f4f87575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528800} :answer-field/id #uuid "0935b382-4574-59fb-a9b6-ea0fdb98be37" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3d0f846-dcec-5a1e-a313-ed34c8131231" :question/math-academy-id "q-355899" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1.11 & -1.51 & -0.99 & -1.19 \\\\ -0.58 & 1.16 & 1.49 & 0.35 \\\\ -0.29 & 0.03 & 0.04 & -0.68 \\\\ 1.43 & 0.57 & -0.84 & 1.42 \\\\ 0.55 & -0.25 & 0.3 & 0.1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.761 & 0.185 & 0.263 & 0.342 & 0.447 \\\\ 0.309 & -0.738 & 0.28 & 0.285 & 0.447 \\\\ -0.169 & -0.143 & -0.022 & -0.866 & 0.447 \\\\ 0.538 & 0.63 & 0.338 & 0.013 & 0.447 \\\\ 0.084 & 0.067 & -0.859 & 0.225 & 0.447\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.125 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.362 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.699 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.455 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.49 & 0.509 & -0.625 & -0.333 \\\\ 0.572 & -0.337 & 0.479 & -0.573 \\\\ 0.25 & -0.761 & -0.55 & 0.237 \\\\ 0.608 & 0.22 & 0.278 & 0.71\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=3.125,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=2.362,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=0.699,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.455.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.125}^{2}}{{3.125}^{2}+{2.362}^{2}+{0.699}^{2}+{0.455}^{2}}≈60.9%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.125}^{2}+{2.362}^{2}}{{3.125}^{2}+{2.362}^{2}+{0.699}^{2}+{0.455}^{2}}≈95.7%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $60.9%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $95.7%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/29/299ec97726335bb0dc02c7d256c7722eb52a7c22cd8b46a856d1b669bbe918dc.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bbf7902-3a15-5dd0-b2c8-e5b7fd541345" :knowledge-point/title "Choosing the Amount of Dimensionality Reduction for a Given Dataset"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fc905bf-a333-5f88-bc94-7532cf83b587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528419} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6ed0a8-2992-50b5-a253-5ba14a0a4402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528597} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe3ce25-405b-53e4-8190-780d13dc0791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528683} {:answer/id #uuid "c7676032-6f77-5131-a597-0e7248c6766a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7676032-6f77-5131-a597-0e7248c6766a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528785} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf9d5ef7-04cb-5f23-a601-17a6256765aa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529040} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "270f63c7-1aeb-558c-8a65-03c3f71ad498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528400} {:answer/id #uuid "3678144c-c5c5-59eb-9c73-7f7cfc99f4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528440} {:answer/id #uuid "8334f0e6-38a4-5448-b6ae-13c404a2e8fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528613} {:answer/id #uuid "bb438be0-e790-540f-8d81-6dbdb5c89782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8334f0e6-38a4-5448-b6ae-13c404a2e8fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528613} :answer-field/id #uuid "d87761c7-3333-5f96-b1b1-7878fa8dd615" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ccff77b-7d01-5bd7-aaf4-9fba844066ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-355903" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.46 & -0.2 & 0.07 & -0.88 \\\\ -1.4 & 0.15 & 0.73 & -0.87 \\\\ -0.24 & 1.53 & 0.68 & 1.35 \\\\ -0.15 & -1.25 & 0.23 & 0.73 \\\\ 1.32 & -0.23 & -1.72 & -0.32\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.186 & 0.257 & 0.252 & -0.792 & -0.456 \\\\ 0.398 & 0.593 & 0.3 & 0.45 & -0.443 \\\\ 0.529 & -0.708 & 0.123 & -0.057 & -0.447 \\\\ -0.015 & 0.115 & -0.887 & 0.015 & -0.446 \\\\ -0.725 & -0.259 & 0.208 & 0.408 & -0.443\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.878 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.152 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.636 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.652 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & -0.419 & 0.045 & -0.68 \\\\ 0.38 & -0.525 & 0.761 & 0.04 \\\\ 0.654 & 0.205 & -0.148 & -0.713 \\\\ 0.262 & -0.712 & -0.63 & 0.166\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=2.878,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=2.152,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1.636,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.652.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.878}^{2}}{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}+{1.636}^{2}+{0.652}^{2}}≈51.7%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}}{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}+{1.636}^{2}+{0.652}^{2}}≈80.6%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first three principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}+{1.636}^{2}}{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}+{1.636}^{2}+{0.652}^{2}}≈97.3%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $51.7%$ of the variance,\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $80.6%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $3$ dimensions would preserve $97.3%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/cc/ccc57a7e978a8b123dd21da151c2b0fec3080be9d39f1c8f26cc3b19c0cbd2b1.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $3\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bbf7902-3a15-5dd0-b2c8-e5b7fd541345" :knowledge-point/title "Choosing the Amount of Dimensionality Reduction for a Given Dataset"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e00e59c-c2e1-5d95-88f6-75a0287bc55a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528417} {:answer/id #uuid "359ded72-a1d6-5847-8db5-fe5d7574386d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528437} {:answer/id #uuid "64e8fa21-4152-565d-bf5a-4add5225dbab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528540} {:answer/id #uuid "8dd94c47-0c6d-5b9d-a0a0-01f30dc5aa0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64e8fa21-4152-565d-bf5a-4add5225dbab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528540} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c994f1c-25ce-53c1-b2bc-ad51547e017a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528999} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b9077d-bc80-505b-8b3d-48730306347d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528347} {:answer/id #uuid "162330ce-ca49-5e79-89b6-d89a13587017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528355} {:answer/id #uuid "beb61f16-161b-5af3-980b-30635c4dc9c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528765} {:answer/id #uuid "c1060128-a39b-5154-8642-ecf7080795dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1060128-a39b-5154-8642-ecf7080795dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528772} :answer-field/id #uuid "8575b8f8-052d-504c-a3e1-9051091e1d1f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "439142f8-6932-5f54-8d84-ca7931d891df" :question/math-academy-id "q-355908" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.36 & 1.7 & 0.25 & 1.05 \\\\ 0.8 & -0.33 & -1.37 & -0.53 \\\\ -0.51 & -0.23 & 0.05 & -0.61 \\\\ -1.18 & -0.96 & -0.32 & -1.01 \\\\ 1.25 & -0.18 & 1.4 & 1.1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.477 & 0.704 & -0.239 & 0.135 & 0.448 \\\\ -0.291 & -0.357 & -0.764 & 0.049 & 0.449 \\\\ -0.217 & 0.106 & 0.24 & -0.83 & 0.442 \\\\ -0.55 & 0.135 & 0.445 & 0.529 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.582 & -0.589 & 0.323 & 0.104 & 0.447\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.041 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.967 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.671 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.268 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.356 & -0.757 & -0.46 & -0.298 \\\\ 0.454 & 0.644 & -0.416 & -0.454 \\\\ 0.493 & -0.1 & 0.783 & -0.366 \\\\ 0.652 & 0.041 & -0.052 & 0.755\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=3.041,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=1.967,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1.671,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.268.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.041}^{2}}{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}+{0.268}^{2}}≈57.9%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}}{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}+{0.268}^{2}}≈82.1%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first three principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}}{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}+{0.268}^{2}}≈99.6%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $57.9%$ of the variance,\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $82.1%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $3$ dimensions would preserve $99.6%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/5e/5eb43d1adcfa48f0fd66cd73ea05b40a9e937399457ba8614f380502c1c632dc.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $3\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204107 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45f11c96-89e5-5662-81b6-06cff80a4cf2" :knowledge-point/title "Using PCA to Dimensionally Reduce an Uncentered Dataset"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c15eb4a1-c754-53ba-aa20-b209310569fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the direction in feature space" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203567} {:answer/id #uuid "573534b3-1f1c-5d7a-a3db-5c47db32aaed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the datapoint" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c15eb4a1-c754-53ba-aa20-b209310569fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499